@startuml
!include ../../../tools/diagrams/kcna-theme.puml
title kubectl run nginx — every step goes through the API server; nobody calls anybody else

rectangle "kubectl\n<size:11>POST /api/v1/namespaces/default/pods</size>" <<plain>> as kubectl
rectangle "kube-scheduler\n<size:11>watching for Pods with no node</size>" <<good>> as sched
rectangle "kube-apiserver\n<size:11>authenticates · authorises · admits · validates</size>\n<size:11>persists to etcd · notifies every watcher</size>" <<accent>> as api
rectangle "etcd\n<size:11>the Pod object, nodeName empty …</size>\n<size:11>then nodeName: worker-1 … then status</size>" <<good>> as etcd
rectangle "kubelet on worker-1\n<size:11>watching for Pods assigned to this node</size>" <<good>> as kubelet
rectangle "containerd → runc\n<size:11>pull nginx · create the sandbox (CNI assigns the IP)</size>\n<size:11>· start the container</size>" <<plain>> as rt

kubectl -down-> api : 1 · create Pod
api -right-> etcd : 2 · write (nodeName empty)
sched -down-> api : 3 · watch fires: unscheduled Pod\n4 · filter + score nodes, write a Binding
api -down-> kubelet : 5 · watch fires: a Pod is assigned here
kubelet -right-> rt : 6 · CRI: run it
kubelet -up-> api : 7 · status: Running, PodIP
kubectl -[hidden]right-> sched

caption <i>The scheduler never talks to the kubelet, and the kubelet never talks to the scheduler. Each watches the API server for the change it cares about and writes back what it did.</i>\n<i>Loose coupling through one shared, watched store is the Kubernetes design pattern — and why every component can restart independently. A Pod with no node yet shows as Pending.</i>
@enduml
